\label{converge to limit set}
Let $\Lambda^P \subset \partial \mathbb{H}$ denote the limit set of $\Gamma^P$.
Then $ \hat{\tau}_\alpha^t$ admits a  canonical extension 
 $ \hat{\tau}_\alpha^t : \mathbb{H} \sqcup \partial \mathbb{H} \rightarrow  \mathbb{H} \sqcup \partial \mathbb{H}$
 which is continuous on $\partial \mathbb{H}$.
 Further: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item For any $w \in \Gamma^P$ one has
$ \hat{\tau}_\alpha^t(w)  = w$;
\item For any $w \in \partial \mathbb{H}$ one has
$ \lim_{t \rightarrow \pm \infty}  \hat{\tau}_\alpha^t(w) \in \Lambda^P$
and further this is an endpoint of an edge of $\partial \overline{P}$.
\end{enumerate}

With the notation above
 we have the following expression for the angle:
\begin{equation}\label{relation}
 \sin^2(\alpha_1 \angle_z \alpha_2) =   
 \frac{
 4( 2 -  \mathrm{tr}
  [\rho(a_1),\rho(a_2) ])
  }
  {( \mathrm{tr}^2 \rho(a_1)-4)( \mathrm{tr}^2 \rho(a_2)-4)}.
 \end{equation}
This equation is actually implicit in  \cite{pjm}
 but it is not claimed to be new there and seems to have been well known.
 The left hand side of  (\ref{relation})
 is clearly an analytic function on $\mathcal{T}(\Sigma)$
 and it follows from elementary real analysis
 the the angle varies real analytically too.
 \hfill $\Box$
